{
  "dom_id": "step-q160944",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $\u0394$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\u0394={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $\u0394$ under the action of $T?$",
  "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $\u0394$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png)",
